# UGAR uncertainty experiments

The five method notebooks score defects in archived code responses from
MBPP, HumanEval, HumanEval-Java, and Defects4J. Each probe is trained and
evaluated independently on each benchmark. Notebook 6 evaluates the
uncertainty-guided agent loop with SAPLMA and UQ Head.

## Setup

From the repository root in PowerShell, use Python 3.12-3.14 and the
project's `.venv`. The notebooks default to an NVIDIA GPU and 4-bit NF4
weights.

```powershell
py -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install torch==2.9.1 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install --no-compile -r requirements.txt
.\.venv\Scripts\hf.exe auth login
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ipykernel install --sys-prefix --name ugar --display-name "Python (UGAR)"
```

Accept the checkpoint's license on Hugging Face before authenticating.
The first run downloads about 16 GB of checkpoint files. Select the
**Python (UGAR)** kernel in Jupyter or select the project's `.venv` in VS
Code. To use unquantized weights, change the notebook to
`TraceStore(quantize=False)` and allow memory for the full model.
[Llama 3.1 8B Instruct](https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct)

## Run the notebooks

| Notebook | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| [MBPP](data_mbpp.ipynb), [HumanEval](data_humaneval.ipynb), [HumanEval-Java](data_humaneval_java.ipynb), [Defects4J](data_defects4j.ipynb) | Dataset examples and split counts |
| [Lookback Lens](01_lookback_lens.ipynb) | Logistic regression on prompt lookback ratios |
| [Uncertainty Quantification Head](02_uq_head.ipynb) | Two-layer Transformer on lookback and recent attention |
| [ICR Probe](03_icr_probe.ipynb) | MLP on layerwise divergence between attention and residual updates |
| [SAPLMA](04_saplma.ipynb) | MLP on activations from decoder block 28 |
| [HaloScope](05_haloscope.ipynb) | Weighted SVD and a binary classifier trained on pseudo-labels from block 16 |
| [Agent review and candidate selection](06_agent_loop.ipynb) | Compare SAPLMA and UQ Head guidance against direct Llama generation |

The saved detector outputs use all 1,198 responses in the Llama 3 subset.
Run all cells to reproduce the tables. Set `pilot = True` for a shorter run
with up to 48 training, 16 validation, and 24 test responses per benchmark.
The archive and model traces are cached in `~/.cache/ugar` and reused by all
five detector methods.

## Agent evaluation

Notebook 6 uses four held-out repair tasks per benchmark. The direct
baseline sends the original task to Llama. Plain review and line-guided
review both get the archived draft and one revision round. Candidate
selection gets the same draft and generates up to three variants in one
call. The direct and review calls allow 256 new tokens. The candidate
generation call allows 768.

The evaluator applies the same fitted probe to the direct and guided
outputs. Line-level PR-AUC and F1 use a diff against the canonical
solution to label unmatched output lines. The F1 threshold comes from the
archived validation split. The uncertainty score delta averages the
per-task maximum code-token score of the guided output minus that of the
direct output. A negative delta means lower estimated uncertainty.
Python pass counts use archived assertions when Docker checks are enabled.

## Data and evaluation

The pinned companion archive pairs Llama 3 8B Instruct generations with
canonical solutions and supplies binary token annotations derived from
diffs. The experiments replay both variants through Llama **3.1** 8B
Instruct. The extractor checks that the tokenizer reproduces the archived
IDs before running the model. Some archived failing generations have zero
positive diff labels and count as clean under the token-level definition.
The results measure defect detection on replayed text.
[Code Hallucination Detection dataset](https://github.com/datapaf/CodeHallucinationDetection/tree/4681508aeec9c7aa491f42308f6090417118a818)

Each benchmark uses a seeded split by task with roughly 60/20/20
proportions. All five methods
use the same examples. A line or sample is defective if it contains any
positive token annotation. Its score is the maximum annotated token
score. The notebooks report trapezoidal PR-AUC, average precision,
ROC-AUC, precision, recall, and F1. The F1 threshold for each level is
selected on validation and applied to the held-out test split.

All methods use the same interface, `fit(traces, labels)` and
`predict_uncertainty(traces)`. HaloScope fits without annotation labels.
The validation thresholds use annotations for every method. The methods
are adapted to token-level defect detection and use one fixed seed.

## Training logs

UQ Head defaults to 10 epochs, ICR Probe to 15, and SAPLMA to 3. The
agent notebook uses each estimator's default. Set `epochs` in an
estimator notebook to override its budget. Each fit records its history in
`estimator.training_history`.
The notebooks enable live logs and display the history as a table.
Training and validation loss are mean binary cross-entropy over labeled
tokens. Validation token PR-AUC and ROC-AUC measure how well the probe
ranks defective tokens. Held-out evaluation uses the final epoch.

## Transformers interface

An extractor instance is callable with a prompt and reply and returns
one row per reply token in each feature tensor. Lookback
and recent-attention features come from the token's prediction position.
ICR and the hidden-state probes use activations after the token is read.
`hidden_states` concatenates selected decoder layers at the prediction
positions. The extractor uses the Transformers checkpoint from the
requested example.

```python
from src.traces import TransformersExtractor

extractor = TransformersExtractor.from_pretrained()
reply = extractor.generate("Who are you?", max_new_tokens=256)
trace = extractor("Who are you?", reply)
hidden = extractor.hidden_states("Who are you?", reply, layers=(-2, -1))
print(reply, trace["saplma"].shape, hidden.shape)
```

`src/traces.py`, `src/methods.py`, and `src/evaluation.py` adapt the neighboring
`llm-uncertainty-layer` repository at commit `811b48a`. The dataset
archive and method papers are linked in the notebooks and this guide.

## Current scope

The completed notebooks cover token-, line-, and response-level metrics,
line ranking, candidate selection, uncertainty-guided review, and estimator
training histories. The agent loop exposes `create_agent` for iterative
line-guided review and `create_agent_candidates` for selecting among up to
three generated code variants. See [RESULTS.md](../RESULTS.md) for the latest
executed pilot results.
